= Pragmatics =

== Overview ==
'''Pragmatics''' studies how language is used and interpreted in context.

Understanding an utterance often requires more than knowing the conventional meanings of its words and syntax. A hearer may also need information about who is speaking, what has already happened in the conversation, shared assumptions, goals, social conventions, and what the speaker is trying to do.

== Semantics and pragmatics ==
[[Semantics]] concerns conventional meaning and interpretation.

Pragmatics concerns context-sensitive use and communication.

The boundary is disputed. Some phenomena that one theory treats semantically may be treated pragmatically by another.

A useful working distinction is:

<pre>
linguistic expression + conventional meaning
                 +
context / intention / discourse state
                 ↓
utterance interpretation
</pre>

This is not a claim that interpretation always occurs in separate stages.

== Context ==
[[Context (linguistics)|Context]] can include:

* surrounding linguistic material;
* speaker and addressee;
* time and place;
* perceptual environment;
* prior discourse;
* [[Common ground|common ground]];
* social and institutional setting;
* conversational goals.

Different pragmatic theories formalize only subsets of this information.

== Major phenomena ==
Pragmatics studies phenomena such as:

* indexicals and deixis;
* reference in use;
* [[Presupposition|presupposition]];
* [[Implicature|implicature]];
* [[Speech act|speech acts]];
* indirect meaning;
* politeness and social meaning;
* information structure;
* conversational organization.

== Speaker meaning ==
A speaker can communicate more than the conventional semantic content of a sentence.

For example, “It is getting late” may simply describe the time, or in context may function as a suggestion to leave.

The same sentence form can participate in different communicative acts.

== Inference ==
Pragmatic interpretation often involves [[Inference|inference]], but it is constrained by language, context, shared knowledge, and communicative expectations.

This does not make pragmatic meaning arbitrary. Competing theories attempt to explain why some interpretations are natural and others are not.

== Epistemic notes ==
There is broad agreement that context and use matter to linguistic interpretation, but major questions remain contested: how much content is semantically encoded, how speaker intentions are recognized, how common ground is represented, and how conversational principles interact with grammar and social practice.

